This example runs a cosmological simulation using 32^3 particles with
the gas density set to the mean baryonic density and no fluctuations
(i.e. sigma_8 == 0). The simulation is run without gravity to prevent
any numerical fluctuation from growing. 

The gas is cooling/heating via its interaction with the UV background
set by the cooling model. In practice, the gas will follow the
equilibirum temperature of the model at all z and only be affected by
reionization. Assuming primoridal abundance, the temperature of the
gas at different redshifts can be compared to temperatures inferred
from observations of the Lyman-alpha forrest. The plotting script runs
this comparison once the simulation has completed.

Within the EAGLE cooling model, interesting changes are to switch
on/off the Helium II reionisation (or change its redshift) as well as
the position and amount of energy injected by Hydrogen
reionisation. The parameters given the YAML file are the ones used for
actual EAGLE galaxy formation runs.

The example can be run with two of the cooling models:

 * EAGLE (Wiersma et al. 2009 tables), configure with
     ./configure --with-cooling=EAGLE --with-chemistry=EAGLE
   The run script fetches the EAGLE cooling tables if they are not present.

 * TREECOOL (Katz, Weinberg & Hernquist 1996 primordial cooling with a
   tabulated UV background), configure with
     ./configure --with-cooling=TREECOOL
   The UV background is read from the TREECOOL_fg_dec11 file shipped with
   this example (Faucher-Giguere et al. 2009, December 2011 update). It
   switches on at z ~ 10.6, the highest redshift of the table, and the gas
   then heats up sharply from the temperature floor to ~10^4 K before
   settling on the photo-heating equilibrium temperature. The bump around
   z ~ 3.5 is the HeII reionization built into the table. As expected for
   an optically thin UV background without any additional heating, the
   temperature stays below the Schaye et al. (2000) measurements around
   z ~ 3 and matches the Walther et al. (2019) ones at z ~ 2-4. Note that
   the SPH minimal temperature of 100 K is ten times the lowest temperature
   of the rate tables, as recommended in the documentation of the model.

The plotting script compares the temperature evolution to observational
constraints in both cases.
